Interviewer = I
Interviewee = S 
Opening (0:00–0:30)

I: Hi there! Thanks so much for taking the time to chat with me today. It’ll just be a quick, casual 10-minute interview about a new club discovery website concept. No right or wrong answers, just share your real thoughts and habits, okay?
S: Sure, no problem at all. Happy to help.
I: Awesome! Let’s get started.
Hypothesis 1: Smart Discovery (Filters) (0:30–2:40)

I: First off, let’s picture this website: you can filter student clubs by personal interest, weekly time commitment, and difficulty level to find suitable clubs really quickly. If you had these three filters right now, could you walk me through how you’d use them to search for a club step by step?
S: Yeah, I’d start with interest first for sure. I’d pick my hobbies or things I want to try, then adjust the time filter next, since I have a busy study schedule. After that, I’d check difficulty to avoid clubs that are way too high-pressure or time-heavy for beginners.
I: That makes sense. Out of interest, time commitment, and difficulty, which one is your top priority when picking a club?
S: Time commitment is definitely the most important for me. My university workload is heavy, so I can’t join anything that takes too much time each week. Interest matters, but if a club is fun but too time-consuming, I still can’t join it.
I: Got it. What small website features would make this filtering process feel super easy and not annoying for you?
S: Simple, clear dropdown menus and slider bars would be great. Also real-time results that update right after I pick a filter, instead of needing to click search every single time.
I: Fair enough. Even with these filters, would there be any parts of the search that still make you feel unsure or confused?
S: Maybe vague difficulty labels. If it just says “easy” or “hard” with no explanation, I still won’t know what to expect. That would leave me confused.
I: Totally get that. Besides filters, what other key info do you need to see to know a club is actually a good fit?
S: Things like member age range, how social the group is, and whether it’s beginner-friendly for new people with no experience.
I: Nice point. Are there any extra filters you wish this site had to speed up your search?
S: I’d love a filter for in-person vs online meetings, and maybe one for casual vs competitive club vibes.
Hypothesis 2: Social-driven Features (2:40–4:50)

I: Alright, moving to the next idea. Imagine this website shows you which of your friends have joined a club or are interested in it. How much would your friends’ club choices influence you to check a new club out?
S: It would influence me a decent amount. If people I know are interested, I’d be way more curious to look into it than a random club I’ve never heard of.
I: Would you ever join a club just because your friends are already in it? Why or why not?
S: I would, honestly. Joining with friends makes new activities less awkward and more fun. Even if the hobby is just okay, hanging out with people I know makes it worth it.
I: Cool. What kind of social or friend activity features would make you want to use this club website more regularly?
S: A simple “friend recommendations” section, and seeing which clubs are popular among people I follow or know. It feels more connected.
I: Do you think seeing your friends’ club activity would push you to try clubs you’d normally never consider on your own?
S: Definitely. I’m pretty picky by myself, but friends can get me to try new things I’d skip otherwise.
I: Last social one: what other social functions would make joining clubs more fun on this platform?
S: Maybe a group chat preview or seeing short member reviews from students, not just official club descriptions.
Hypothesis 3: Clear Club Information (4:50–7:00)

I: Great. Next scenario: every club page clearly lists regular activities, meeting times, membership rules, and what being a member is actually like day-to-day. What’s the single most important detail you need to see on a club profile before you even think about joining?
S: Fixed meeting times and frequency. I need to know exactly when it runs to plan my class and study schedule around it.
I: Do you think typical university club info is clear right now? What’s usually missing or confusing?
S: It’s usually really messy. Most only post basic meeting times and skip club culture, member expectations, or if there’s extra fees. A lot of key details are hidden.
I: How would clean, well-organized club info help you make a faster decision?
S: I wouldn’t have to message club leaders back and forth to ask basic questions. I can scan the page in 30 seconds and decide yes or no right away.
I: Got it. What details about club culture or community do you wish every club profile included?
S: Whether the group is quiet and laid-back or super outgoing, and if they welcome shy new members. Club culture matters a lot for comfort.
I: What specific profile details would make you 100% confident a club is perfect for you?
S: Clear time schedules, no hidden fees, beginner support, and honest descriptions of weekly tasks and activities.
Hypothesis 4: Easy Joining Process (7:00–9:00)

I: Perfect, last concept. The website has a super simple step-by-step online sign-up, and you can finish your club application in just a few minutes. How would a quick joining process change your motivation to sign up for clubs?
S: It would make me way more likely to join. Long, complicated forms make me give up halfway. Fast sign-up removes that laziness barrier.
I: In this ideal easy process, what’s the minimum steps or basic info you think is necessary to join?
S: Just my student ID, a short interest reason, and agreeing to basic club rules. No long essays or unnecessary personal questions.
I: Are there any annoying joining steps, like physical paperwork or long waiting periods, you’d want to remove completely?
S: Definitely physical forms and waiting weeks for approval. Everything should be online with fast confirmation.
I: Even if the process is simple, what might still stop you from finishing the sign-up?
S: If it asks for too much personal data, or if the page glitches and crashes mid-form. Small technical issues kill the momentum.
I: How would you design the most smooth, enjoyable joining experience for students?
S: Short one-page forms, progress indicators, and an instant confirmation email after submitting. Keep it quick and stress-free.
Interview Closing (9:00–10:00)

I: That’s all our main questions! I really appreciate you sharing all your honest thoughts on filters, social features, club info and the joining process. Your feedback is super helpful for this project.
S: No worries at all, it was a relaxed chat. I hope it helps!
I: It absolutely does. Just one final quick question: is there anything else you want to add about finding and joining university clubs that we didn’t talk about today?
S: Not really, I think we covered all the main points I care about when looking for clubs.
I: Wonderful. Thanks again for your time and cooperation. Have a nice rest of your day!
S: You too, thank you!
